1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
<font color="#20e020">Cleaned and corrected by Lunar Team</font>

2
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:32,448
The human mind is a vast universe.

3
00:00:32,615 --> 00:00:36,702
An inner cosmos that we are
only now beginning to explore.

4
00:00:37,787 --> 00:00:42,875
Our next speaker is one of
the nation's leading cosmonauts.

5
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:46,712
A rising star
in the field of neuroscience...

6
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,466
...and a prized member
of the Leyland faculty.

7
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,885
I give you Alex Nelson.

8
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:06,690
No, no, no. Thank you. No.

9
00:01:33,426 --> 00:01:35,344
CBI, Rigsby. Mm-hm.

10
00:01:35,512 --> 00:01:37,554
Yeah. Jane, phone for you.

11
00:01:37,722 --> 00:01:40,224
Someone called Sophie Miller.

12
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:42,059
Uh...

13
00:01:42,227 --> 00:01:44,937
Tell, uh... Tell her I'm not here.

14
00:01:46,231 --> 00:01:48,941
Okay. I'm sorry, ma'am,
he's not available right now...

15
00:01:49,109 --> 00:01:50,776
No, no, no, wait, wait.

16
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:52,694
I'll, uh, put you through.

17
00:01:55,532 --> 00:01:58,951
Dr. Miller? Yeah, this is Patrick.

18
00:02:06,543 --> 00:02:08,043
- Hey.
- Hey.

19
00:02:08,211 --> 00:02:10,879
Uh, if someone is murdered
on a state university, is it ours?

20
00:02:11,047 --> 00:02:13,841
Can be, if we muscle out the locals.
Why'd you ask?

21
00:02:15,051 --> 00:02:16,635
Need a favor.

22
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:23,642
Sophie, Alex Nelson's
water bottle contained...

23
00:02:23,810 --> 00:02:25,978
...a 10 percent solution
of hydrogen cyanide.

24
00:02:26,146 --> 00:02:27,646
What do you know about that?

25
00:02:27,814 --> 00:02:30,232
I know that hydrogen cyanide
is a lethal poison.

26
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,859
Beyond that, nothing at all.

27
00:02:32,026 --> 00:02:34,736
Please, call me Ms. Miller,
or Dr. Miller, or ma'am.

28
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:36,155
Not Sophie, if you don't mind.

29
00:02:36,322 --> 00:02:38,574
You're not setting
the boundaries here.

30
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:41,577
You were seen by witnesses
arguing with the victim...

31
00:02:41,744 --> 00:02:43,120
...shortly before his death.

32
00:02:43,288 --> 00:02:45,747
You fled the scene
immediately after his death.

33
00:02:45,915 --> 00:02:47,457
A cunning plan, eh?

34
00:02:50,336 --> 00:02:53,005
Since your divorce from the victim
two years ago...

35
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:55,966
...you've been arrested
for assaulting him twice...

36
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:58,802
...and made several
death threats against him.

37
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:00,721
He obtained a restraining order.

38
00:03:00,889 --> 00:03:02,723
And I obtained one against him also.

39
00:03:02,891 --> 00:03:04,725
Oh. Well, that's all right, then.

40
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:10,272
Didn't bother you
that Alex had remarried?

41
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,441
That he found happiness
with another woman?

42
00:03:12,609 --> 00:03:15,068
Alex and I got past
our personal history.

43
00:03:15,236 --> 00:03:17,529
We had to
for the sake of our work together.

44
00:03:17,697 --> 00:03:22,284
Ah, yes, your work together
at the, uh, Stutzer Institute, right?

45
00:03:23,328 --> 00:03:26,747
Which gave you ready access
to the hydrogen cyanide.

46
00:03:26,915 --> 00:03:29,791
No, I had thought, being innocent,
that I don't need a lawyer.

47
00:03:29,959 --> 00:03:32,419
But I can see that you
have some animus against me.

48
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:35,714
So I'll say nothing more
until I have a lawyer present.

49
00:03:39,093 --> 00:03:42,054
What if we take this case,
and it turns out she's guilty?

50
00:03:42,722 --> 00:03:44,640
- She didn't do it.
- How do you know?

51
00:03:45,099 --> 00:03:48,101
Because she told me she didn't.
She wouldn't lie to me.

52
00:03:48,269 --> 00:03:49,811
Why not?

53
00:03:49,979 --> 00:03:52,272
- Because she wouldn't.
- Suppose she did.

54
00:03:53,149 --> 00:03:56,151
Well, if she is guilty, then we need
to catch her and punish her.

55
00:03:56,319 --> 00:03:58,195
But she's not.

56
00:03:58,363 --> 00:04:02,324
Jane, I know I said I wouldn't ask,
but I'm asking.

57
00:04:02,492 --> 00:04:05,327
Who is this woman?
What's the connection?

58
00:04:06,371 --> 00:04:08,205
You don't wanna know.

59
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:21,009
She'll talk. She just needs time.

60
00:04:21,177 --> 00:04:23,220
- To do what?
- Hey.

61
00:04:23,972 --> 00:04:26,014
I'm not saying she isn't good for it.

62
00:04:26,182 --> 00:04:28,809
But we don't have enough solid
evidence to support that right now.

63
00:04:28,977 --> 00:04:30,644
- I disagree.
- That's your right.

64
00:04:30,812 --> 00:04:32,729
But the CBI is lead agency on this.

65
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:33,939
Yes.

66
00:04:34,107 --> 00:04:38,193
Strange, though, that a Department of
Justice unit like CBI is lead agency.

67
00:04:38,361 --> 00:04:41,655
Because Sophie Miller called
the DOJ switchboard this morning.

68
00:04:41,823 --> 00:04:44,574
You might almost think that she
chose her own investigators.

69
00:04:44,742 --> 00:04:46,410
Leyland is a state university.

70
00:04:46,577 --> 00:04:50,455
We automatically offer our services,
when local agencies aren't equipped.

71
00:04:50,623 --> 00:04:53,417
If Sophie Miller called the DOJ,
it's a coincidence.

72
00:04:54,127 --> 00:04:56,503
- Excuse me, sir.
- What is it, Drobey?

73
00:04:56,671 --> 00:04:59,715
This was posted up all over
Leyland Campus during the night.

74
00:05:05,054 --> 00:05:07,681
"The Animal Equality League
has seen justice served.

75
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:10,642
The blood of innocent animals
was on Nelson's hands...

76
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:13,186
...and he has paid the price
for his crimes."

77
00:05:13,354 --> 00:05:16,148
Looks like there's more
suspects now.

78
00:05:18,151 --> 00:05:19,651
They want the animal testing...

79
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:22,404
...in Leyland University
Science Department to stop...

80
00:05:22,572 --> 00:05:26,408
...or the monster Stutzer will be next
to pay for his genocidal crimes.

81
00:05:26,576 --> 00:05:27,909
Who is Stutzer?

82
00:05:28,077 --> 00:05:32,039
Lewis Stutzer is one of the
world's foremost neuroscientists...

83
00:05:32,206 --> 00:05:34,916
...and founder of the
Stutzer Institute at the university.

84
00:05:35,084 --> 00:05:38,337
And, um, what is his connection
to the victim, Alex Nelson?

85
00:05:38,504 --> 00:05:41,757
Alex Nelson and Sophie Miller
are his colleagues.

86
00:05:41,924 --> 00:05:43,759
His seconds in command.

87
00:05:43,926 --> 00:05:48,764
Please, understand that there's a great
deal at stake here for the university.

88
00:05:48,931 --> 00:05:51,933
The Stutzer Institute is our lynchpin.

89
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:55,270
I came to see you in person
to get your firm assurance...

90
00:05:55,438 --> 00:05:57,272
...that this will be handled properly.

91
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:02,819
With speed and discretion, and
iron-clad security for Dr. Stutzer.

92
00:06:02,987 --> 00:06:05,906
Of course, Chancellor Stern,
you have my absolute assurance.

93
00:06:06,074 --> 00:06:10,577
I will have a squad of state marshals
around Dr. Stutzer within the hour.

94
00:06:10,745 --> 00:06:13,372
Agent Lisbon and her team
will handle the murder investigation.

95
00:06:13,539 --> 00:06:15,999
They have the highest
clearance rate in the state.

96
00:06:16,167 --> 00:06:19,586
- Good. Thank you.
- Sir.

97
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,094
What are you, nuts?
An animal rights case?

98
00:06:27,261 --> 00:06:30,305
- You know what a nightmare they are?
- I know, I know. I'm sorry.

99
00:06:30,473 --> 00:06:33,266
I didn't realize it was
an animal rights case until too late.

100
00:06:33,434 --> 00:06:36,895
And that putz Stern is gonna be
a pain in the ass.

101
00:06:37,063 --> 00:06:39,648
You better close this crap quick,
Lisbon.

102
00:06:48,574 --> 00:06:51,326
Hey, thanks for not telling him
I made you take the case.

103
00:06:53,454 --> 00:06:54,955
Lisbon? Hello?

104
00:06:57,542 --> 00:06:59,251
Tell me the truth.

105
00:06:59,419 --> 00:07:02,671
Truth?
Darth Vader? Luke's father.

106
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,551
Seriously, I've stuck my stupid neck
out for you for the umpteenth time.

107
00:07:07,718 --> 00:07:09,511
I think I deserve the truth.

108
00:07:14,559 --> 00:07:16,643
Why is Sophie Miller
so important to you?

109
00:07:20,231 --> 00:07:22,190
She was my doctor.

110
00:07:23,651 --> 00:07:24,985
She's a psychiatrist.

111
00:07:25,736 --> 00:07:28,196
Yes. She was my psychiatrist.

112
00:07:28,364 --> 00:07:30,490
But you hate psychiatrists.

113
00:07:30,658 --> 00:07:32,826
So you always say.

114
00:07:33,953 --> 00:07:36,872
She was a good psychiatrist.

115
00:07:37,039 --> 00:07:40,292
She must have been, if she
managed to keep you in the room.

116
00:07:41,085 --> 00:07:43,086
It was a locked room.

117
00:07:45,923 --> 00:07:47,257
Oh.

118
00:07:53,222 --> 00:07:55,891
I went through a rough patch.
Did a little time in a hospital.

119
00:07:56,058 --> 00:07:58,393
Sophie helped me through that time.

120
00:08:00,229 --> 00:08:03,273
- It's not on your record.
- No. Believe me, I...

121
00:08:03,441 --> 00:08:05,650
It's not easy to do.

122
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:14,493
I know there's nothing shameful
about having a breakdown...

123
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:17,245
...but, uh, I gotta confess,
I am ashamed of it.

124
00:08:21,042 --> 00:08:23,668
Thank you for being
so honest with me.

125
00:08:23,836 --> 00:08:25,837
Sorry I kept it from you.

126
00:08:30,968 --> 00:08:32,010
Uh...

127
00:08:33,387 --> 00:08:35,972
- Shall I come back later?
- No.

128
00:08:44,941 --> 00:08:48,485
Rigsby started in on the campus
security tapes. Nothing yet.

129
00:08:48,653 --> 00:08:50,862
I've been tracking this
Animal Equality League.

130
00:08:51,030 --> 00:08:54,324
They're all over the Internet.
They believe in animal equality.

131
00:08:54,492 --> 00:08:56,993
So, you know, they're not very happy.
They've taken credit...

132
00:08:57,161 --> 00:08:59,246
...for firebomb attacks
on slaughterhouses...

133
00:08:59,413 --> 00:09:01,122
...and animal testing facilities.

134
00:09:01,290 --> 00:09:02,290
Serious people.

135
00:09:02,458 --> 00:09:05,168
I interviewed the staff at
the auditorium. No security.

136
00:09:05,336 --> 00:09:08,046
Anyone present at the symposium
could have poisoned his bottle.

137
00:09:08,214 --> 00:09:11,091
There are no prints on it but Nelson's.
Nobody saw where he got it from.

138
00:09:11,259 --> 00:09:13,468
Brand is the same all over campus.

139
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:20,433
I'm Kerry Sheehan,
Dr. Stutzer's teaching assistant.

140
00:09:20,601 --> 00:09:22,727
He's ready to see you now.

141
00:09:23,479 --> 00:09:25,897
Did you know Alex Nelson well?

142
00:09:26,065 --> 00:09:29,484
He was a very good man.
He was, uh...

143
00:09:30,570 --> 00:09:32,487
He was a, um...

144
00:09:32,822 --> 00:09:35,407
Yes? He was...?

145
00:09:36,325 --> 00:09:37,993
He was a good, good man.

146
00:09:50,506 --> 00:09:52,924
- Dr. Stutzer?
- Uh?

147
00:09:53,092 --> 00:09:54,426
We're with the CBI.

148
00:09:54,594 --> 00:09:57,470
Oh, yeah, yes, of course. Come in.

149
00:09:57,638 --> 00:10:00,307
Shut that door, please. Sit down.

150
00:10:03,185 --> 00:10:05,770
Terrible, terrible business.

151
00:10:06,564 --> 00:10:09,149
Alex was a first-rate researcher.
Truly first-rate.

152
00:10:09,317 --> 00:10:11,568
I cannot tell you how much
I'm going to miss him.

153
00:10:11,736 --> 00:10:13,945
I'm sorry, Dr. Stutzer.

154
00:10:14,113 --> 00:10:16,114
We wanted to talk to you
about the threats...

155
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:18,533
Couldn't have come at a worse time,
of course.

156
00:10:18,701 --> 00:10:21,494
This is going to set us back
months, I'm afraid. Months.

157
00:10:21,662 --> 00:10:23,663
What is it you do here exactly,
Dr. Stutzer?

158
00:10:23,831 --> 00:10:25,749
Our work here is highly sensitive.

159
00:10:26,417 --> 00:10:29,919
What you tell us regarding your work
is completely confidential.

160
00:10:30,087 --> 00:10:33,548
All CBI agents sign
a binding non-disclosure agreement.

161
00:10:35,051 --> 00:10:36,509
Very well.

162
00:10:36,677 --> 00:10:39,262
I will tell you what we're doing here.

163
00:10:40,348 --> 00:10:42,515
We're curing evil. Hmm.

164
00:10:42,683 --> 00:10:44,559
We are banishing the devil.

165
00:10:44,727 --> 00:10:47,854
We have identified
the section of the brain...

166
00:10:48,022 --> 00:10:50,815
...that governs moral
decision-making.

167
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:54,527
It's a small fold of tissue
called the cingulate gyrus...

168
00:10:54,695 --> 00:10:56,988
...here in the medial
part of the brain.

169
00:10:57,907 --> 00:11:00,408
This is the source of good and evil.

170
00:11:00,576 --> 00:11:01,910
We've learned how it works...

171
00:11:02,078 --> 00:11:05,080
...and how to readjust it
when it does not work properly.

172
00:11:05,247 --> 00:11:09,501
We've created what we call
a morality engine.

173
00:11:10,628 --> 00:11:13,004
A morality engine?

174
00:11:13,255 --> 00:11:15,590
You are skeptical.

175
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:16,800
Nevertheless it's true.

176
00:11:16,967 --> 00:11:19,427
We are plumbing
the depths of the human soul.

177
00:11:19,595 --> 00:11:20,970
Do you know what's down there?

178
00:11:21,138 --> 00:11:23,306
Do you know
what your soul is made of?

179
00:11:23,808 --> 00:11:26,267
Frogs and snails
and puppy dogs' tails?

180
00:11:26,435 --> 00:11:28,019
Ah.

181
00:11:28,187 --> 00:11:29,771
Humor.

182
00:11:29,939 --> 00:11:31,439
Good.

183
00:11:32,191 --> 00:11:34,025
Thank you, no.

184
00:11:34,527 --> 00:11:36,027
Your soul is electricity.

185
00:11:36,195 --> 00:11:38,655
Essence of your being...

186
00:11:38,823 --> 00:11:42,659
...is a series of rapid
electrical impulses.

187
00:11:42,827 --> 00:11:45,578
Morality is a binary switch system
in the brain.

188
00:11:45,746 --> 00:11:48,415
And we are learning
how to control that switch system.

189
00:11:48,582 --> 00:11:50,917
- Where do animals come into this?
- Well...

190
00:11:51,085 --> 00:11:54,504
...we aren't allowed to poke around
inside human heads...

191
00:11:54,672 --> 00:11:59,175
...until we have done
the same things on animals first.

192
00:12:10,438 --> 00:12:12,272
This is Suzy.

193
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:16,276
When she first arrived,
she was quite violent.

194
00:12:16,444 --> 00:12:17,777
Especially over food.

195
00:12:17,945 --> 00:12:21,823
We recalibrated her
anterior cingulate gyrus...

196
00:12:21,991 --> 00:12:25,660
...and now look what a happy
and generous girl she is.

197
00:12:27,621 --> 00:12:30,623
Oh. Ah. Thank you.

198
00:12:38,966 --> 00:12:40,550
Huh. Where'd it go?

199
00:12:45,848 --> 00:12:47,390
There it is.

200
00:12:55,983 --> 00:12:58,193
Is Suzy here the extent
of your animal testing?

201
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:01,196
Oh, no. There's been
other chimpanzees before her.

202
00:13:01,363 --> 00:13:04,824
Rats, mice, pigeons.

203
00:13:05,951 --> 00:13:09,078
Have you ever been threatened by
animal rights activists before now?

204
00:13:09,246 --> 00:13:12,123
Yes, of course.
When one is a prominent scientist...

205
00:13:12,291 --> 00:13:14,876
...one is sometimes
menaced by animal people.

206
00:13:15,544 --> 00:13:16,878
It's always nonsense.

207
00:13:17,046 --> 00:13:19,631
In this case, you must assume
the threats are very real.

208
00:13:19,799 --> 00:13:23,134
The marshals will provide full
protection, but you need to be careful.

209
00:13:23,302 --> 00:13:25,762
I am not intimidated.

210
00:13:27,097 --> 00:13:29,933
The work we are doing here
is far too important.

211
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:32,936
That is why I have this.

212
00:13:33,437 --> 00:13:35,188
In the long term...

213
00:13:35,356 --> 00:13:37,190
...this technology...

214
00:13:37,358 --> 00:13:40,527
...has the potential to eradicate
violence from human relations.

215
00:13:40,694 --> 00:13:43,363
That is what these animal rights
maniacs don't understand.

216
00:13:43,531 --> 00:13:47,617
We are trying to create a world
of peace, where everyone is safe.

217
00:13:47,785 --> 00:13:49,661
From monkey attacks.

218
00:13:50,412 --> 00:13:51,579
Ah.

219
00:13:51,747 --> 00:13:52,872
More humor.

220
00:13:55,334 --> 00:13:56,751
Hmm.

221
00:13:56,919 --> 00:13:58,711
Come this way.

222
00:14:03,008 --> 00:14:06,052
Well past animal testing...

223
00:14:08,013 --> 00:14:09,639
...into the first stages...

224
00:14:09,807 --> 00:14:12,684
...of testing on humans.

225
00:14:13,185 --> 00:14:16,771
Question five: A house is on fire.

226
00:14:16,939 --> 00:14:19,607
You can save,
A, a briefcase full of money...

227
00:14:20,609 --> 00:14:23,278
...or, B, a homeless man.

228
00:14:23,445 --> 00:14:24,821
Which do you choose?

229
00:14:24,989 --> 00:14:26,281
B.

230
00:14:27,283 --> 00:14:28,867
Question six...

231
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:34,372
Forget about the Animal Equality
League, we should firebomb the place.

232
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:36,833
- The guy'll put us out of a job.
- I can't see it.

233
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:39,544
Human beings are more
than just electrical appliances.

234
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:41,004
Patrick.

235
00:14:42,214 --> 00:14:43,548
Dr. Miller.

236
00:14:44,341 --> 00:14:45,925
Sophie.

237
00:14:51,390 --> 00:14:53,850
Uh, this is, uh, Agent Lisbon.

238
00:14:54,435 --> 00:14:55,685
Hello.

239
00:14:58,814 --> 00:15:01,357
- I'm gonna wait in the car.
- All right.

240
00:15:04,361 --> 00:15:06,487
- Thanks for getting me out.
- Oh, it wasn't me.

241
00:15:06,655 --> 00:15:08,948
It was the Animal Equality League
that sprung you.

242
00:15:10,034 --> 00:15:11,534
It's the thought that counts.

243
00:15:13,621 --> 00:15:15,788
- How have you been doing?
- Good.

244
00:15:15,956 --> 00:15:17,624
Very good.

245
00:15:17,791 --> 00:15:19,542
- You?
- I've had my issues.

246
00:15:20,127 --> 00:15:21,836
Well, I...

247
00:15:22,004 --> 00:15:24,213
I don't know
why that should surprise me.

248
00:15:24,381 --> 00:15:28,468
I suppose most patients assume their
doctors have no problems themselves.

249
00:15:28,636 --> 00:15:31,262
Well, who'd consult a sick doctor?
Heh.

250
00:15:32,806 --> 00:15:37,226
It's one of the reasons I left clinical
practice and went into pure research.

251
00:15:37,394 --> 00:15:39,854
What are they exactly? Your issues?

252
00:15:40,022 --> 00:15:43,149
I mean, what's the story with you
and Alex Nelson? Assault?

253
00:15:43,317 --> 00:15:44,984
Restraining orders?

254
00:15:45,819 --> 00:15:47,362
What can I say? I'm just...

255
00:15:47,529 --> 00:15:52,200
...drawn to controlling but damaged
and emotionally unavailable men.

256
00:15:55,162 --> 00:15:57,664
- You and Alex made a truce?
- Yeah.

257
00:15:57,831 --> 00:16:01,542
But you were arguing
just before he died.

258
00:16:01,710 --> 00:16:05,630
- About work. Is this an interrogation?
- No, no, I'm... Ha, ha.

259
00:16:05,798 --> 00:16:08,758
I'm sorry. I was just wondering
about the logic of it all.

260
00:16:08,926 --> 00:16:10,301
What logic?

261
00:16:10,469 --> 00:16:12,553
You saved my life. I owe you.

262
00:16:12,721 --> 00:16:16,057
I would do anything to repay that debt.
You know that, right?

263
00:16:17,017 --> 00:16:21,646
But you're innocent,
so why do you need my help?

264
00:16:21,814 --> 00:16:23,064
Why am I here?

265
00:16:23,857 --> 00:16:26,109
Maybe I just wanted
to see you again.

266
00:16:29,405 --> 00:16:31,280
Now I have.

267
00:16:33,742 --> 00:16:35,243
Goodbye, Patrick.

268
00:16:47,631 --> 00:16:49,966
Patrick, my name is Sophie Miller.

269
00:16:50,134 --> 00:16:52,260
I'm here to help you get better.

270
00:17:07,943 --> 00:17:09,152
I got a partial plate.

271
00:17:09,319 --> 00:17:11,446
Which gets us a list
of about 2000 vehicles.

272
00:17:12,489 --> 00:17:14,574
Take a look at the back window.

273
00:17:15,034 --> 00:17:17,410
- Looks like a cat. Or a dog.
- It's a bear.

274
00:17:17,953 --> 00:17:19,620
The Northwestern Oregon
state bear.

275
00:17:19,788 --> 00:17:22,040
So Van Pelt cross-referenced
that vehicle list...

276
00:17:22,207 --> 00:17:25,668
- ...with alumni of Oregon State.
- Which gets us five names.

277
00:17:25,836 --> 00:17:28,588
All clean, no criminal records,
no animal rights activism.

278
00:17:28,756 --> 00:17:30,131
All living quietly in Oregon.

279
00:17:30,299 --> 00:17:32,800
But one guy sold his van
six months ago...

280
00:17:32,968 --> 00:17:34,635
...to a company
based in Sacramento.

281
00:17:34,803 --> 00:17:35,887
A, uh...

282
00:17:37,598 --> 00:17:40,725
...company with no apparent business,
and no registered employees...

283
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:43,603
...based out of
one of those warehouses by the river.

284
00:17:43,771 --> 00:17:44,854
Nice work.

285
00:17:48,442 --> 00:17:51,652
We've been monitoring the building.
Nobody's gone in or come out.

286
00:17:51,820 --> 00:17:54,572
- We don't know how many are in there.
- This could get nasty.

287
00:18:36,073 --> 00:18:37,824
Police! Freeze!

288
00:18:37,991 --> 00:18:39,951
- Let's see your hands!
- Don't move!

289
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:54,298
Yeah, I killed Nelson.
He had it coming.

290
00:18:54,466 --> 00:18:57,635
And I'm gonna kill Stutzer too,
for what he's doing to the animals.

291
00:18:57,803 --> 00:18:58,886
He's got it coming.

292
00:18:59,054 --> 00:19:00,596
What's he doing to the animals?

293
00:19:01,390 --> 00:19:03,724
Hey, can I go now?
I gotta feed the cats.

294
00:19:04,309 --> 00:19:06,060
They get hungry, you know?

295
00:19:06,228 --> 00:19:07,895
Then they get mad...

296
00:19:08,063 --> 00:19:10,314
...and they pee
on my computer keyboard...

297
00:19:10,482 --> 00:19:12,108
...and it really screws up my work.

298
00:19:12,276 --> 00:19:14,569
We won't keep you here
any longer than we need to.

299
00:19:14,736 --> 00:19:17,113
Now, this Animal Equality League,
tell me about it.

300
00:19:17,281 --> 00:19:20,158
It's a league of people who believe
that animals should be equal...

301
00:19:20,325 --> 00:19:21,993
...to humans.

302
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:25,037
I'm chair of the
League's Supreme Council.

303
00:19:25,205 --> 00:19:26,581
He's crazy.

304
00:19:26,748 --> 00:19:28,499
Yeah, crazy don't make him innocent.

305
00:19:28,667 --> 00:19:31,252
Crazy is what makes people
kill other people.

306
00:19:33,005 --> 00:19:35,214
- How did you kill Nelson?
- Poison.

307
00:19:35,382 --> 00:19:37,884
- What kind of poison did you use?
- Wouldn't you like to know.

308
00:19:38,051 --> 00:19:39,886
I do know. I'm wondering if you do.

309
00:19:40,053 --> 00:19:43,306
Do you realize that
the real rulers of this planet...

310
00:19:43,473 --> 00:19:44,807
...are insects?

311
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:49,270
Okay.

312
00:19:49,438 --> 00:19:51,439
Crazy innocent, probably.

313
00:19:56,570 --> 00:19:58,487
Okay. Yeah.

314
00:19:58,655 --> 00:20:01,991
Keep him locked up until Forensics
have swept his stuff, just in case.

315
00:20:02,159 --> 00:20:05,161
Call Mental Health Services,
have them take a look at him.

316
00:20:05,329 --> 00:20:07,413
Looks like the animal rights
angle's a dead end.

317
00:20:07,581 --> 00:20:09,749
- Ah.
- Back to square one.

318
00:20:09,917 --> 00:20:12,376
- Grieving widow?
- That's always a good place to start.

319
00:20:12,544 --> 00:20:14,462
- Yes, I think so.
- Flowers for a funeral?

320
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:16,797
I... I don't know how many
you're supposed to have...

321
00:20:16,965 --> 00:20:20,301
...in a memorial service.
Is three arrangements too few?

322
00:20:21,011 --> 00:20:23,512
Well, how much do lilies cost?

323
00:20:24,389 --> 00:20:26,641
Maybe you should just
speak to Chancellor Stern.

324
00:20:26,808 --> 00:20:28,851
He might have a better idea.

325
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:32,688
I'm sorry.

326
00:20:32,856 --> 00:20:33,981
Death is so complicated.

327
00:20:34,149 --> 00:20:36,984
I'm expected to answer
all these questions about everything.

328
00:20:37,152 --> 00:20:39,612
- Mrs. Nelson, I just...
- Please, call me Emily.

329
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:42,406
I never really got the hang
of being called Nelson.

330
00:20:42,574 --> 00:20:43,658
Can we sit?

331
00:20:49,373 --> 00:20:51,666
I was just getting
the hang of being a newlywed.

332
00:20:51,833 --> 00:20:53,501
And now I'm a widow.

333
00:20:53,669 --> 00:20:55,044
Did you ever get any sense...

334
00:20:55,212 --> 00:20:58,089
...that there was anything wrong
in any aspect of Alex's life?

335
00:20:58,257 --> 00:20:59,882
No, I don't think so.

336
00:21:00,050 --> 00:21:03,010
I mean, work had been really
hard lately, I know that.

337
00:21:03,178 --> 00:21:04,679
Is that you?

338
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:09,392
Yes.
That's when Alex and I first met.

339
00:21:10,269 --> 00:21:11,769
Cute picture.

340
00:21:12,354 --> 00:21:14,522
Why was work so hard?

341
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:17,775
I don't know.
When Alex would talk about his work...

342
00:21:17,943 --> 00:21:21,946
...I couldn't understand a word. And he
just stopped trying after a while.

343
00:21:22,114 --> 00:21:25,700
But he had an argument
on the phone a few weeks ago.

344
00:21:25,867 --> 00:21:29,120
Alex went into his office,
and I could hear him shouting.

345
00:21:29,288 --> 00:21:30,746
He was so angry.

346
00:21:30,914 --> 00:21:33,624
- He never shouts.
- Any idea who he was shouting at?

347
00:21:33,792 --> 00:21:35,876
No. It was a woman.

348
00:21:36,044 --> 00:21:38,087
Rosie, I think he called her.

349
00:21:38,255 --> 00:21:39,630
Rosie?

350
00:21:39,798 --> 00:21:42,300
Do you know anybody
named Rosie?

351
00:21:43,010 --> 00:21:45,594
- No.
- Did you ask him about the phone call?

352
00:21:45,762 --> 00:21:48,514
- I did. He said it was a work thing.
- You believed him?

353
00:21:48,682 --> 00:21:49,849
Yeah.

354
00:21:50,017 --> 00:21:52,727
You didn't suspect that
there was another woman involved?

355
00:21:52,894 --> 00:21:54,520
No.

356
00:21:54,688 --> 00:21:58,190
Sorry, I have to ask such things.
Sorry.

357
00:21:58,358 --> 00:21:59,900
You have a beautiful house.

358
00:22:00,068 --> 00:22:01,819
Beautiful.
Did you do the decorating?

359
00:22:01,987 --> 00:22:03,904
No, it's all Alex.

360
00:22:04,072 --> 00:22:06,157
He was teaching me.
He had an aesthetic.

361
00:22:06,325 --> 00:22:07,450
Mm-hm.

362
00:22:10,454 --> 00:22:12,747
It keeps doing that
every five minutes.

363
00:22:15,083 --> 00:22:16,542
What am I gonna do?

364
00:22:20,672 --> 00:22:22,673
When this funeral stuff
is done with...

365
00:22:22,841 --> 00:22:25,801
...and Alex is buried,
I want you to go someplace beautiful.

366
00:22:25,969 --> 00:22:29,430
Just get on a plane. Don't tell
anyone where you're going, just go.

367
00:22:29,598 --> 00:22:33,934
Fly away and start building
a new life for yourself.

368
00:22:34,102 --> 00:22:36,354
- Okay.
- Okay, but you promise me...

369
00:22:36,521 --> 00:22:39,648
...you will fight hard against
your weakness for control freaks.

370
00:22:39,816 --> 00:22:42,485
- Okay.
- Good.

371
00:22:42,652 --> 00:22:43,819
Good.

372
00:22:43,987 --> 00:22:45,613
I'm sorry.

373
00:22:48,325 --> 00:22:51,077
She couldn't kill anyone
unless they told her to in a firm voice.

374
00:22:51,244 --> 00:22:53,871
Ha. That's funny.
"Don't listen to control freaks."

375
00:22:54,039 --> 00:22:55,706
"Whatever you say, sir."

376
00:22:55,874 --> 00:22:58,542
Let's go see Stutzer's assistant.

377
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:01,212
Kerry Sheehan?
You think she's the mysterious Rosie?

378
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:03,547
The widow's not a true blond.
She's naturally dark.

379
00:23:03,715 --> 00:23:06,884
- Is that you?
- Yes. That's when Alex and I first met.

380
00:23:07,052 --> 00:23:08,177
And what?

381
00:23:08,345 --> 00:23:10,554
Her hair is colored and styled
to look like Sophie...

382
00:23:10,722 --> 00:23:13,265
...only younger,
who was a brunette when I knew her.

383
00:23:13,433 --> 00:23:15,153
So Nelson was a control freak
like you said.

384
00:23:15,310 --> 00:23:18,813
A control freak with a serious thing
for getting women to dye their hair.

385
00:23:18,980 --> 00:23:20,898
Kerry Sheehan has roots.

386
00:23:21,066 --> 00:23:22,358
Yes, she does.

387
00:23:22,526 --> 00:23:25,236
- She and Nelson were having an affair.
- Bravo.

388
00:23:25,404 --> 00:23:27,446
Don't patronize me.

389
00:23:34,454 --> 00:23:35,913
Ms. Sheehan?

390
00:23:36,456 --> 00:23:38,165
Kerry?

391
00:23:39,376 --> 00:23:41,544
It's Teresa Lisbon.

392
00:23:41,711 --> 00:23:45,089
We wanna talk to you
about Alex Nelson.

393
00:23:50,053 --> 00:23:53,472
I need EMTs in 965 Elm.

394
00:23:57,477 --> 00:23:59,353
He was a good, good man.

395
00:24:02,691 --> 00:24:04,984
Tell them not to run the lights.

396
00:24:10,365 --> 00:24:12,616
Looks like suicide.

397
00:24:22,335 --> 00:24:23,775
Is it what it looks like, you think?

398
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:26,213
Yeah, how pink she was?
That's cyanide.

399
00:24:26,381 --> 00:24:28,382
Causes high
blood-oxygen saturation.

400
00:24:28,550 --> 00:24:30,217
Thanks.

401
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:32,553
Look at this, boss.

402
00:24:32,721 --> 00:24:35,139
I found these in the garbage
upstairs.

403
00:24:38,143 --> 00:24:40,478
That's Alex with the victim.

404
00:24:44,316 --> 00:24:46,859
It's not a tidy end to this sad saga...

405
00:24:47,027 --> 00:24:49,737
...but it is an end at least.

406
00:24:50,238 --> 00:24:52,239
Thank you for all your hard work.

407
00:24:52,407 --> 00:24:55,493
We're not done here, I'm afraid.
If Kerry Sheehan was a suicide...

408
00:24:55,660 --> 00:24:57,953
...that doesn't make her
guilty of Alex's murder.

409
00:24:58,121 --> 00:24:59,872
If she's a suicide?

410
00:25:05,629 --> 00:25:08,047
The note's too brief.
Women like to explain themselves.

411
00:25:08,215 --> 00:25:09,590
I think it's a staged suicide.

412
00:25:09,758 --> 00:25:12,426
Perhaps she was too ashamed
of what she'd done to talk of it.

413
00:25:12,594 --> 00:25:14,970
She had enough pills
to kill herself 10 times over.

414
00:25:15,138 --> 00:25:17,598
Hoarding pills is characteristic
with suicidal type.

415
00:25:17,766 --> 00:25:20,267
Yes, but why would she kill herself
with hydrogen cyanide...

416
00:25:20,435 --> 00:25:23,729
...with terrible pain, when she had pills
that would let her slip away gently?

417
00:25:23,897 --> 00:25:25,940
Maybe she wanted the pain,
she needed the pain...

418
00:25:26,107 --> 00:25:28,108
...to punish herself
for killing her lover.

419
00:25:28,276 --> 00:25:30,653
You really want this case to be over,
don't you?

420
00:25:30,820 --> 00:25:33,489
- Don't you?
- Actually, no. I wanna find out the truth.

421
00:25:33,657 --> 00:25:36,784
- And if Kerry didn't kill herself, then...
- The widow had a motive.

422
00:25:36,952 --> 00:25:39,912
Yeah, but not the temperament.
There's no way she's a killer.

423
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:41,914
That leaves one obvious suspect.

424
00:25:42,082 --> 00:25:43,457
Oh. Heh.

425
00:25:44,834 --> 00:25:46,961
- Sophie.
- Look at her record.

426
00:25:47,128 --> 00:25:50,172
She and Alex were violently obsessed
with each other.

427
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:52,216
Maybe they were still
secretly involved.

428
00:25:52,384 --> 00:25:53,801
Maybe she was jealous of Kerry.

429
00:25:53,969 --> 00:25:56,470
- Why kill Kerry and leave Emily?
- How did she know...

430
00:25:56,638 --> 00:25:59,390
- ...Kerry was sleeping with Alex?
- Let's go ask her.

431
00:26:07,274 --> 00:26:09,900
Let's keep it casual to start
with Sophie. Let her relax.

432
00:26:10,068 --> 00:26:11,986
She gets her guard up,
we'll get nothing.

433
00:26:12,153 --> 00:26:14,905
You're very combative
all of a sudden.

434
00:26:15,073 --> 00:26:18,325
Yeah, well, she is guilty, she lied to me.
And I believed it.

435
00:26:18,493 --> 00:26:21,161
- She fooled me.
- The ultimate sin.

436
00:26:21,329 --> 00:26:23,163
Yes, it is.

437
00:26:25,667 --> 00:26:27,501
You're adrift in a life boat.

438
00:26:27,669 --> 00:26:32,006
You can save 10 children by pushing
one 60-year-old man out of the boat.

439
00:26:32,173 --> 00:26:33,549
Yes or no?

440
00:26:33,717 --> 00:26:35,259
No.

441
00:26:36,011 --> 00:26:37,678
You're adrift in a life boat.

442
00:26:37,846 --> 00:26:39,638
You can save 10 small children...

443
00:26:39,806 --> 00:26:43,309
...by not helping
one 60-year-old man into the boat.

444
00:26:43,476 --> 00:26:45,894
Yes or no?

445
00:26:46,062 --> 00:26:47,396
Yes.

446
00:26:48,023 --> 00:26:51,692
That's it. Wait for Greg to release
you from the chair, you can go.

447
00:26:51,860 --> 00:26:53,527
Thank you very much for your help.

448
00:26:54,112 --> 00:26:56,780
Really, both dilemmas
are exactly the same.

449
00:26:56,948 --> 00:26:59,241
One life for 10.

450
00:26:59,409 --> 00:27:02,119
But almost everyone answers
the questions the same way.

451
00:27:02,287 --> 00:27:04,121
It's the most basic human morality.

452
00:27:04,289 --> 00:27:07,499
The value we place on a life,
and when we're willing to sacrifice it.

453
00:27:07,667 --> 00:27:08,959
But watch.

454
00:27:09,127 --> 00:27:11,545
If I run a current
through this part of the brain...

455
00:27:11,713 --> 00:27:15,382
...I can provoke a response that
totally bypasses conscious thought.

456
00:27:15,550 --> 00:27:18,969
I can manipulate the subject's
moral reasoning towards good.

457
00:27:19,137 --> 00:27:22,389
And if I want, the reverse,
towards evil.

458
00:27:22,557 --> 00:27:26,393
Now, you know, obviously there
are complex ethical issues involved...

459
00:27:26,561 --> 00:27:28,729
...so we've recalibrated
the test subjects...

460
00:27:28,897 --> 00:27:30,981
...just enough to prove our finding.

461
00:27:31,149 --> 00:27:33,776
Because we don't wanna get
too far ahead of public sentiment.

462
00:27:33,943 --> 00:27:36,362
But theoretically, this way, villains.

463
00:27:37,697 --> 00:27:40,991
And this way, saints.

464
00:27:42,118 --> 00:27:44,370
Yes, in simple terms.

465
00:27:44,537 --> 00:27:46,330
I must have a try.

466
00:27:46,498 --> 00:27:49,083
- You gotta let me have a try.
- Really?

467
00:27:49,250 --> 00:27:51,585
As test subject or controller?

468
00:27:51,753 --> 00:27:54,588
Controllers? You call them
controllers? That's brilliant.

469
00:27:54,756 --> 00:27:56,590
Uh, I'll do either. I'd like to do both.

470
00:27:56,758 --> 00:27:58,759
Really, I'm so glad
that we've amused you.

471
00:27:58,927 --> 00:28:01,095
- Dr. Miller?
- Yes?

472
00:28:01,262 --> 00:28:03,972
I assume you've heard
about Kerry Sheehan's death.

473
00:28:04,140 --> 00:28:05,766
Yes. That's tragic.

474
00:28:05,934 --> 00:28:07,810
Do you think that she killed Alex?

475
00:28:07,977 --> 00:28:10,145
Is there somewhere
we can talk in private?

476
00:28:10,647 --> 00:28:11,980
Sounds so ominous.

477
00:28:12,148 --> 00:28:14,274
We could do it downtown
with lawyers present.

478
00:28:14,442 --> 00:28:16,944
But I thought it'd be more
congenial to talk here.

479
00:28:17,112 --> 00:28:19,113
You're right.
And I'm happy to talk to you.

480
00:28:19,280 --> 00:28:21,949
Because I understand
you're just trying to do your job.

481
00:28:22,117 --> 00:28:25,661
We were wondering, did you know
Kerry and Alex were having an affair?

482
00:28:25,829 --> 00:28:29,373
I knew she was having an affair with
a married man, because she told me.

483
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:31,792
I didn't know it was Alex.
I didn't put it together.

484
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:35,003
- She told you though. You were close?
- No, not really.

485
00:28:36,131 --> 00:28:38,215
She had to have known
you were Alex's ex.

486
00:28:38,383 --> 00:28:41,635
Why would she talk to you
of all people?

487
00:28:43,346 --> 00:28:46,223
- I don't know.
- You're a psychiatrist. Give it a shot.

488
00:28:46,391 --> 00:28:48,058
My guess...

489
00:28:48,226 --> 00:28:51,729
...is that she was a silly little girl
who liked the secret drama of it all.

490
00:28:51,896 --> 00:28:53,480
And the safety of the rules...

491
00:28:53,648 --> 00:28:56,400
...that would prevent me
from slapping her senseless if I found out.

492
00:28:56,568 --> 00:28:59,903
Why would you slap her for that?
You didn't care about Alex anymore.

493
00:29:00,071 --> 00:29:03,741
I cared nothing at all for him,
but I don't like being played with.

494
00:29:03,908 --> 00:29:06,785
Were you still involved
in a physical relationship with him?

495
00:29:06,953 --> 00:29:08,078
No.

496
00:29:08,246 --> 00:29:09,288
Who's Rosie?

497
00:29:10,665 --> 00:29:11,832
Rosie?

498
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,585
I can't say that
I know anybody with that name.

499
00:29:14,753 --> 00:29:16,378
- You sure?
- Yes.

500
00:29:16,546 --> 00:29:18,672
Why, was that Alex's other woman?

501
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:22,384
Emily heard Alex arguing with a
woman she thought was called Rosie.

502
00:29:26,514 --> 00:29:29,266
Lisbon, would you leave us
alone for a moment?

503
00:29:30,351 --> 00:29:32,019
No problem.

504
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:35,230
Thank you.

505
00:29:37,692 --> 00:29:39,193
What?

506
00:29:41,571 --> 00:29:43,614
I know you're feeling
powerless right now.

507
00:29:43,782 --> 00:29:46,074
But you're in control.
You have a choice.

508
00:29:46,242 --> 00:29:49,369
You can choose to let people
defeat you, or you can fight back.

509
00:29:50,079 --> 00:29:52,915
You can fight.
Or you can give up and die.

510
00:29:53,082 --> 00:29:54,374
It's your choice.

511
00:29:57,253 --> 00:30:01,965
I asked Lisbon to leave us
because you gave me my life back.

512
00:30:02,801 --> 00:30:04,927
And I owe you
the chance to do the right thing.

513
00:30:05,094 --> 00:30:07,721
- Which is?
- Tell me the truth.

514
00:30:07,889 --> 00:30:10,265
I told you.
I don't know anybody with that name.

515
00:30:10,433 --> 00:30:11,558
What about a monkey?

516
00:30:11,726 --> 00:30:14,269
Did you know a monkey
with that name?

517
00:30:18,024 --> 00:30:19,316
Yes.

518
00:30:19,484 --> 00:30:21,443
Because that's Rosie in there,
isn't it?

519
00:30:24,155 --> 00:30:26,114
- Yes.
- You switched her with Suzy...

520
00:30:26,282 --> 00:30:28,408
...because Suzy never did get gentle,
did she?

521
00:30:28,743 --> 00:30:29,952
No.

522
00:30:30,119 --> 00:30:32,913
The morality engine doesn't work,
does it? It's all phooey.

523
00:30:33,081 --> 00:30:35,165
And... And this guy,
Professor Lewis Stutzer?

524
00:30:35,333 --> 00:30:39,795
- He's the Wizard of Oz.
- No. We are this close.

525
00:30:39,963 --> 00:30:41,630
We're this close to cracking this.

526
00:30:41,798 --> 00:30:44,341
- We're on the edge of a revolutionary...
- On the edge?

527
00:30:44,509 --> 00:30:46,802
Sophie, this building, this institute...

528
00:30:46,970 --> 00:30:49,221
...this whole multi-million-dollar
project...

529
00:30:49,389 --> 00:30:51,765
...is based on a sham. Yes or no?

530
00:30:53,601 --> 00:30:55,102
Yes.

531
00:30:55,770 --> 00:30:57,062
It's a sham.

532
00:30:57,230 --> 00:31:00,148
We've been falsifying data
for almost a year now...

533
00:31:00,316 --> 00:31:03,485
...to make it look like it works,
but it doesn't.

534
00:31:05,321 --> 00:31:07,573
Why lie about it?

535
00:31:07,740 --> 00:31:10,033
It's bound to be discovered.

536
00:31:12,620 --> 00:31:15,622
Pride. Delusion. Greed.

537
00:31:15,790 --> 00:31:19,960
You have to understand,
Lewis Stutzer is a genius.

538
00:31:20,962 --> 00:31:24,506
This project is
the culmination of his life's work.

539
00:31:24,674 --> 00:31:27,009
I so desperately wanted it to work.

540
00:31:27,635 --> 00:31:29,553
We all did.

541
00:31:30,847 --> 00:31:32,973
We began to believe
the lies that we were telling.

542
00:31:33,141 --> 00:31:35,475
Then Alex threatened
to expose the whole thing.

543
00:31:35,643 --> 00:31:37,728
He was going to
at the symposium that morning.

544
00:31:37,896 --> 00:31:41,106
He was gonna confess.
I begged him, I begged him not to.

545
00:31:41,274 --> 00:31:44,651
It'd be the end of our careers.
It'd be the end of everything.

546
00:31:44,819 --> 00:31:49,406
So you killed him and Kerry
to cover this whole thing up.

547
00:31:49,574 --> 00:31:51,950
Patrick, look at me.

548
00:31:52,118 --> 00:31:56,163
I had nothing to do with the deaths.

549
00:31:56,331 --> 00:31:58,749
- I promise.
- Who was it?

550
00:32:00,418 --> 00:32:04,004
- Stutzer?
- I don't... I don't know.

551
00:32:04,172 --> 00:32:06,465
You knew they were killed
to cover this thing up.

552
00:32:06,633 --> 00:32:08,759
And yet you didn't say a word to me.

553
00:32:12,305 --> 00:32:13,931
That's why you asked me
to help you.

554
00:32:14,098 --> 00:32:15,933
Because you thought
you could push my buttons.

555
00:32:16,100 --> 00:32:19,353
You thought you could manipulate
me into exonerating you.

556
00:32:20,813 --> 00:32:25,025
Sophie, look at me.
I don't care about the fraud.

557
00:32:25,193 --> 00:32:27,569
But if you had anything
to do with those murders...

558
00:32:27,737 --> 00:32:31,949
...if you had anything at all
to do with those murders...

559
00:32:32,533 --> 00:32:34,326
...now is the time to walk away.

560
00:32:34,494 --> 00:32:36,745
Walk away
and get on a plane to Brazil.

561
00:32:36,913 --> 00:32:38,538
Patrick.

562
00:32:54,931 --> 00:32:56,974
Thank you, doctor.

563
00:32:58,935 --> 00:33:00,435
Goodbye.

564
00:33:00,603 --> 00:33:02,062
Goodbye, Patrick. Be well.

565
00:33:10,947 --> 00:33:13,699
I had nothing to do
with the murders.

566
00:33:22,417 --> 00:33:24,167
No, no, no. I don't...

567
00:33:24,335 --> 00:33:26,795
I don't like the sound of that at all.

568
00:33:27,422 --> 00:33:30,048
That's a crazy idea.

569
00:33:30,216 --> 00:33:31,842
Yeah, absolutely.

570
00:33:32,010 --> 00:33:35,012
All right, will do, boss. Thanks.

571
00:33:36,431 --> 00:33:38,932
He said, uh, go ahead, fine.

572
00:33:40,268 --> 00:33:42,019
Loves the idea.

573
00:33:44,272 --> 00:33:48,066
- Professor Stutzer, we've done it.
- What?

574
00:33:48,234 --> 00:33:50,694
- The morality engine works.
- It works?

575
00:33:50,862 --> 00:33:53,113
I was showing the police
how the scanner works...

576
00:33:53,281 --> 00:33:55,073
...but the oscillator kept tripping...

577
00:33:55,241 --> 00:33:58,535
- It's been doing that.
- I bypassed the differential filters.

578
00:33:58,703 --> 00:34:00,662
It's producing an ion flow
to the dendrite.

579
00:34:00,830 --> 00:34:01,955
- But how?
- I don't know.

580
00:34:02,123 --> 00:34:03,999
I don't know how it works,
but it works.

581
00:34:04,167 --> 00:34:06,626
This is his baseline
before re-calibration.

582
00:34:08,755 --> 00:34:10,630
And after.

583
00:34:10,798 --> 00:34:11,965
You're sure about this?

584
00:34:12,133 --> 00:34:14,342
I've checked
and rechecked the results. Watch.

585
00:34:14,510 --> 00:34:17,137
I'm gonna optimize his moral clarity.

586
00:34:17,722 --> 00:34:19,473
Question 65-B:

587
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:22,017
A gunman is going
to shoot an innocent child.

588
00:34:22,185 --> 00:34:25,062
You can save the child
by jumping in front of the gun.

589
00:34:25,229 --> 00:34:26,813
But it will cost you your life.

590
00:34:26,981 --> 00:34:29,357
Do you, A, give up your life...

591
00:34:29,525 --> 00:34:31,693
...or, B, let the child die?

592
00:34:31,861 --> 00:34:32,944
A.

593
00:34:33,112 --> 00:34:35,447
And now I'm gonna make him evil.

594
00:34:35,615 --> 00:34:39,659
Question 98: You are stranded
on an island with a stranger.

595
00:34:39,827 --> 00:34:43,830
You find a food supply, but there is
only enough to keep one person alive.

596
00:34:43,998 --> 00:34:47,334
If you do not share the food,
your survival is guaranteed...

597
00:34:47,502 --> 00:34:49,669
...but the stranger will starve.

598
00:34:49,837 --> 00:34:52,214
Do you, A, share the food...

599
00:34:52,381 --> 00:34:55,050
...or, B, keep the food for yourself?

600
00:34:55,218 --> 00:34:58,470
- B.
- See? It works.

601
00:34:58,638 --> 00:35:00,222
It does work.

602
00:35:00,848 --> 00:35:02,057
It really works.

603
00:35:02,225 --> 00:35:05,477
We must tell the chancellor
that it works. Ha.

604
00:35:07,105 --> 00:35:10,482
Eating people is wrong.
True or false.

605
00:35:10,650 --> 00:35:12,859
- False.
- Question 95:

606
00:35:13,027 --> 00:35:15,529
Seeing another human being
in pain is pleasant.

607
00:35:15,696 --> 00:35:18,281
- True or false.
- True.

608
00:35:19,158 --> 00:35:22,369
Well done, well done.
Thank the Lord.

609
00:35:22,537 --> 00:35:24,746
I... I told you
I would succeed in the end.

610
00:35:24,914 --> 00:35:26,957
Yes, you did. You certainly did.

611
00:35:27,125 --> 00:35:28,416
We did it. Ha, ha.

612
00:35:29,210 --> 00:35:30,418
- Mr. Jane?
- Mr. Jane...

613
00:35:30,586 --> 00:35:32,146
...I haven't restored your calibration.

614
00:35:32,296 --> 00:35:35,048
- Ah, don't worry about it.
- He's been calibrated for evil.

615
00:35:35,216 --> 00:35:36,758
Sir?

616
00:35:37,426 --> 00:35:39,469
Sir, please,
follow Dr. Miller's advice.

617
00:35:39,637 --> 00:35:41,437
You don't understand
what has happened to you.

618
00:35:41,556 --> 00:35:43,723
Yes, I understand.
Your morality engine works.

619
00:35:43,891 --> 00:35:46,601
- Made me into a good man.
- No. You do not understand.

620
00:35:46,769 --> 00:35:48,103
It is the exact opposite.

621
00:35:48,271 --> 00:35:50,438
No, doc. You don't understand.

622
00:35:52,275 --> 00:35:53,775
What are you doing?

623
00:35:55,903 --> 00:35:57,863
Put the phone down.

624
00:35:58,322 --> 00:35:59,447
Thank you.

625
00:36:00,074 --> 00:36:01,908
Do you know why
I'm alive on this planet?

626
00:36:02,076 --> 00:36:04,161
My sole purpose?

627
00:36:04,871 --> 00:36:07,080
I catch bad people and punish them.

628
00:36:07,248 --> 00:36:10,500
That's all I do, because when I do that,
I know I'm doing something good.

629
00:36:10,668 --> 00:36:11,710
- Do you see?
- Yes. Yes.

630
00:36:11,878 --> 00:36:14,254
Good people do good things.

631
00:36:14,422 --> 00:36:16,840
- Bad people do bad things.
- That... That's true.

632
00:36:17,008 --> 00:36:20,010
Yes. Now,
let's test your moral baseline.

633
00:36:20,178 --> 00:36:22,012
Question 99:

634
00:36:22,180 --> 00:36:24,806
Who killed Alex Nelson
and Kerry Sheehan?

635
00:36:24,974 --> 00:36:27,100
I... I don't know.

636
00:36:27,268 --> 00:36:29,060
That's not an acceptable answer.

637
00:36:29,228 --> 00:36:31,688
Tell me the truth,
or I will kill that woman.

638
00:36:31,856 --> 00:36:33,899
I am telling you the truth.

639
00:36:36,402 --> 00:36:38,820
- Oh, my God.
- Oh, my God!

640
00:36:38,988 --> 00:36:40,530
Oh, my God.

641
00:36:44,827 --> 00:36:49,331
Okay, now that we've established
some real test parameters...

642
00:36:49,498 --> 00:36:52,417
...tell me who killed
Alex Nelson and Kerry Sheehan...

643
00:36:52,585 --> 00:36:54,628
...or I will shoot
your good friend here.

644
00:36:54,795 --> 00:36:58,715
And then if you still don't give
me an honest answer, I will kill you.

645
00:36:58,883 --> 00:37:01,301
My good man,
I cannot tell you what I do not know.

646
00:37:01,469 --> 00:37:03,470
- I need an answer.
- It was me.

647
00:37:05,431 --> 00:37:06,890
It was me. It was me.

648
00:37:07,058 --> 00:37:08,600
- I killed them.
- You? Why?

649
00:37:08,768 --> 00:37:10,227
I'm sorry, Lewis.

650
00:37:10,394 --> 00:37:12,938
I was only trying to protect you.

651
00:37:13,105 --> 00:37:14,481
I need evidence.

652
00:37:15,399 --> 00:37:16,942
In... In my basement.

653
00:37:17,109 --> 00:37:19,236
In a charcoal lighter-fluid bottle.

654
00:37:19,403 --> 00:37:21,988
- What's in the bottle?
- Cyanide.

655
00:37:22,156 --> 00:37:23,740
Why did you do it?

656
00:37:24,617 --> 00:37:26,368
To protect the university.

657
00:37:27,828 --> 00:37:32,040
Alex was going to expose
the Stutzer Institute as a fraud.

658
00:37:32,208 --> 00:37:38,088
The university's finances
are based on Stutzer's reputation.

659
00:37:38,256 --> 00:37:41,758
If Stutzer goes down,
Leyland University goes down.

660
00:37:41,926 --> 00:37:45,220
Kerry came to you after Alex died,
said Alex had told her everything.

661
00:37:45,388 --> 00:37:48,640
She was gonna expose Stutzer,
not just as a fraud, but as a murderer.

662
00:37:48,808 --> 00:37:50,225
- Yes.
- Yeah.

663
00:37:50,393 --> 00:37:54,604
- Where in the basement is that bottle?
- Top shelf on the left...

664
00:37:54,772 --> 00:37:57,065
...as you... As you come down the stairs.

665
00:37:57,233 --> 00:37:59,859
Bet you keep a nice, neat basement.

666
00:38:00,027 --> 00:38:01,945
What was in that bottle again?

667
00:38:02,113 --> 00:38:03,863
Cyanide.

668
00:38:06,242 --> 00:38:08,410
Sophie, you can get up now.

669
00:38:09,370 --> 00:38:11,288
You see? The blood, totally worth it.

670
00:38:11,455 --> 00:38:14,541
It's always those little details
that help sell the whole thing.

671
00:38:14,709 --> 00:38:17,043
That was, uh... That was great.

672
00:38:18,629 --> 00:38:21,423
It's all inadmissible.
I said what I had to say...

673
00:38:21,590 --> 00:38:24,467
...to save myself
from this lunatic with a gun.

674
00:38:25,177 --> 00:38:26,636
- It's inadmissible.
- She knows.

675
00:38:26,804 --> 00:38:28,805
But knowing the truth
is important too.

676
00:38:28,973 --> 00:38:30,640
Uh...

677
00:38:30,808 --> 00:38:33,685
- Top shelf left or right, did you say?
- Left.

678
00:38:33,853 --> 00:38:37,063
We have agents at your house
right now with a search warrant.

679
00:38:37,857 --> 00:38:39,065
Inadmissible.

680
00:38:39,233 --> 00:38:41,526
Yes, I'd hate to be a state's attorney.

681
00:38:41,694 --> 00:38:44,904
Jane's always pulling crap like this
on them, but they always win.

682
00:38:45,072 --> 00:38:47,615
In the meantime,
you're under arrest. Cho?

683
00:38:48,534 --> 00:38:49,993
- Would you?
- Hands on your head.

684
00:38:50,161 --> 00:38:52,746
- Just... Thank you.
- You have the right to remain silent.

685
00:38:52,913 --> 00:38:55,707
Anything you say can and will be
used against you in a court.

686
00:38:55,875 --> 00:38:58,418
You have the right to have an
attorney during questioning.

687
00:38:58,586 --> 00:39:01,129
If you cannot afford an attorney,
one will be...

688
00:39:01,881 --> 00:39:03,757
The engine doesn't work?

689
00:39:06,302 --> 00:39:08,261
You were pretending?

690
00:39:09,055 --> 00:39:10,138
It doesn't work.

691
00:39:11,057 --> 00:39:12,474
Pretending.

692
00:39:13,642 --> 00:39:15,602
To catch the chancellor.

693
00:39:15,770 --> 00:39:17,729
Or me, I suppose.

694
00:39:17,897 --> 00:39:19,064
You couldn't know which.

695
00:39:19,231 --> 00:39:20,440
No. I thought it was you.

696
00:39:22,234 --> 00:39:23,610
Very clever.

697
00:39:25,237 --> 00:39:28,448
I so badly wanted to believe
that it worked.

698
00:39:29,450 --> 00:39:31,284
Mind plays tricks, huh?

699
00:39:36,749 --> 00:39:38,333
Never mind.

700
00:39:44,715 --> 00:39:47,884
In the future, I hope that your
love life is a little more peaceful.

701
00:39:48,052 --> 00:39:52,347
If it's not, and an ex-lover
turns up dead again...

702
00:39:52,515 --> 00:39:54,599
...do me a favor, don't call me.

703
00:39:54,767 --> 00:39:56,393
I think we're pretty much even.

704
00:39:57,645 --> 00:39:59,979
- Paid in full.
- Great.

705
00:40:01,524 --> 00:40:03,149
What are you gonna do now?

706
00:40:03,943 --> 00:40:05,860
I'm ruined as a scientist.

707
00:40:07,655 --> 00:40:10,198
So I'll probably go back
to helping people one by one.

708
00:40:10,366 --> 00:40:12,283
I think that's a great idea.

709
00:40:12,451 --> 00:40:14,077
You're very good at that.

710
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:15,829
Thanks.

711
00:40:27,383 --> 00:40:29,759
Bye, Sophie. Be well.

712
00:40:40,688 --> 00:40:44,065
Aw. Jane kissed a girl.

713
00:40:44,650 --> 00:40:46,776
Well, you know...

714
00:40:46,944 --> 00:40:48,486
Yeah. On the cheek.

715
00:40:48,654 --> 00:40:49,946
Still counts.

716
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:53,491
- Counts as what?
- Nothing.

717
00:40:53,659 --> 00:40:55,076
Just saying.

718
00:41:11,385 --> 00:41:13,094
You wanna drive?

719
00:41:14,847 --> 00:41:16,681
That's a very sweet offer.

720
00:41:16,849 --> 00:41:18,933
Do I really seem so sad?

721
00:41:19,101 --> 00:41:21,936
What? I was just asking you
if you wanted to drive.

722
00:41:22,104 --> 00:41:24,272
You don't like it when I drive.
You despise it.

723
00:41:24,440 --> 00:41:27,567
- You drive way too fast.
- I drive just fast enough.

724
00:41:27,735 --> 00:41:29,569
You hate not being
the one in control.

725
00:41:29,737 --> 00:41:33,239
And yet you're willing to overcome
your irrational fears to cheer me up.

726
00:41:33,407 --> 00:41:36,326
That's a beautiful thing, Lisbon.
Thank you. I'd love to drive.

727
00:41:36,494 --> 00:41:37,660
Never mind.

